<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1946 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kangding Love Song</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="zh">康定情歌</bdi> / <bdi lang="zh-Latn">Kāngdìng Qínggē</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A love song from the town of Kangding in China, written down in 1946 and now sung all over the Chinese-speaking world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional; scored by Wu Wen-ji</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the people of Kangding, in the former Sikang Province</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kangding, Sichuan, China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>collected and scored 1946</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (a traditional song of Kangding, first written down, scored and named by Wu Wen-ji in 1946)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Kangding Qingge&quot;, the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangding-qingge">Kangding Love Song</a>&quot;, is a traditional folk song of Kangding, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> Province, and one of the most popular songs across the Sinosphere.</p><p>In 1946 Wu Wen-ji, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> who was studying vocal music in Sikang Province, collected a local folk song called &quot;Paoma Liuliude Shanshang&quot; (&quot;On the Running Horse Mountain&quot;) among others. While teaching at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> military academy he wrote out its score and renamed it &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangding-qingge">Kangding Love Song</a>&quot;, after Kangding, the capital of Sikang Province. The soprano Yu Yixuan, popular at the time, performed it, and through her it spread to the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>It has since been performed or recorded by many artists, among them Plácido Domingo, Timi Zhuo and A-mei. It has been sampled and reworked in popular music, film, television and video games: Karen Mok's &quot;The Smooth Love <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; samples it, Tan Weiwei remixed it on the television contest I Am a Singer in 2015, Sammo Hung sings part of it in the film Dragons Forever, and it is heard in an episode of the television series Daredevil.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A folk love song, named after the town of Kangding. Its mood is light and lilting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
